  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSunday and Sunday night, confidence continues to increase that a \nshortwave will dig into the Great Lakes and send a weakening cold \nfront south and into our area. Forcing along this front does not \nappear to be particularly strong, as it becomes far removed from the \nshortwave and surface low as it moves through our area. As such, it \nwill likely lose momentum as it arrives, and there is some \nuncertainty regarding both its timing and potential to drive another \nround of showers and thunderstorms. Precipitation probabilities \nremain modest as a result (20-40%), but Sunday afternoon/evening \nrepresents the next best opportunity for showers and storms.\n\nMuch more likely is that this front will bring heat relief to the \nmajority of the area (with a few possible exceptions) early next \nweek, although this may not completely be felt until Monday across \nthe Ozarks. Given the timing uncertainty we have opted not to extend \nany heat headlines into Sunday just yet, but that potential does \nexist mainly south of I-70. There is also a very real possibility \nthat this front will stall somewhere near our southern fringes and \nwash out, which may prevent our far south (Reynolds, Iron, Madison, \netc.) from truly seeing substantial heat relief before perhaps a \nbrief rebound on Wednesday. Meanwhile, rain chances diminish behind \nthe front thanks to a push of drier air, although rain chances may \nlinger in the vicinity of the front wherever it stalls. By mid week \n(WED/THU) a reinforcing shortwave is likely to send another front \ninto our area, renewing modest chances for showers and \nthunderstorms, and reinforcing heat relief late in the week.",
